R-PA4 R-PA5 R-PA6 R-PA10 R-PA13 R-SP6 R-SP23 R-LF4 R-BL1 R-BL2 R-CM11 R-LB2 R-AL6 R-EC10 R-EC14 R-CC2 R-CC7 R-EC17 R-OUT2 (slice 043, a patched entry in the commands that resolve): 13 runs on 10 projects with the fake index and the fake probes (they pass). The world: acme/pf 0.1.0 (the platform), acme/json 2.1.0 (the base of the patch), 2.2.0 and 2.3.0, all in the cache (the pick reads their headers).
The copy of the patch (in `.talon/patches/json/` of p1 to p7) is the base with 4 changes: 2 lines of `Json.roc` changed, `Old.roc` deleted, `New.roc` added, and the binary file `data.bin` changed. json 2.2.0 has 3 lines more at the top of `Json.roc` (the hunks move by 3 lines) and a new file `Extra.roc`: the diff applies. json 2.3.0 changes line 3 of `Json.roc`, the text of `Old.roc` and the bytes of `data.bin`: the diff does not apply (3 files).
Run 1 (p1, `update`): the patched entry is held, with the note `json: patched, held` (R-PA4). Nothing changes, so no probe runs and nothing is written. Run 2 (p1, `upgrade`): the same note (R-PA4, R-SP6). Run 3 (p1, `install`): the base of the patch is the hash of the header entry, so nothing is rebased (R-PA6).
Run 4 (p2, `update json`): the named entry is free (R-PA4). The highest release is 2.3.0: the diff does not apply, the release is not a candidate, the tuple is dropped (`note: tuple dropped: ...` with the 3 files and the 2 fixes), and json 2.2.0 is picked (R-PA5). The diff is applied to 2.2.0 in a temp dir (the files of the temp tree are written, deleted and copied from the copy). The probes: the baseline check has `--replace-dep <json 2.1.0> <the copy>`, the candidate check and the build have `--replace-dep <json 2.2.0> <the temp dir>` (R-PA10). The header is written, then the copy is replaced by the temp tree and `base` is the hash of json 2.2.0 (R-PA5). The temp dirs are deleted (R-CC2). The `tree.diff` of p2 shows the new copy (`Json.roc` has the 3 lines, `Extra.roc` is new, `Old.roc` stays deleted, `data.bin` has the bytes of the copy).
Run 5 (p3, `update json@^2.3`, key `^2.1`): the selector leaves only json 2.3.0. The diff does not apply: no tuple, exit 10, nothing is written (R-PA5). The text lists the 3 files and names the 2 fixes: edit the copy, or remove the `[patch]` entry.
Run 6 (p4, `update json --dry-run`): the same as run 4 with nothing written: the copy and `talon.toml` stay, the temp dirs are deleted, the lines are the same, and the last line is `dry run: nothing was written` (R-LF4).
Run 7 (p5, `install --frozen`): the header was edited by hand to json 2.2.0 and the base is json 2.1.0: a stale patch. Exit 14, nothing is written (R-PA6). Run 8 (p6, `install`): the same project: `tln install` applies the patch to the header release (json 2.2.0) in a temp dir, replaces the copy and sets `base`. The header does not change, and no probe runs (no header change, R-AL6) (R-PA6).
Run 9 (p1, `verify`): the patch is held, and the probes (the baseline check, the candidate check and the build, `plan_replace`) have `--replace-dep <json 2.1.0> <the copy>` (R-PA10). Run 10 (p7, `update json --no-check`): the diff is applied and the copy is replaced; no probe runs (`check: skipped`, R-BL6).
Run 11 (p8, `update json`): the same as run 4, but an editor changes the `base` of the `[patch]` entry of `talon.toml` while the probes run (`fx/hooks`, after the 10th `probes` call of the case). The second read of `talon.toml` has another text in the span that Talon edits (the entry of `json` in `[patch]`): conflict, exit 17, nothing is written (R-CC7, R-EC17). The header, the copy and `talon.toml` have the editor's text only, and the temp dirs are deleted (R-CC2). The new `base` is written in the same read, check and write as the keys of `talon.toml`, not by a read of its own.
Run 12 (p9, `update json`): `talon.toml` has the comment `# keep`, and an editor changes it while the probes run (after the 12th `probes` call, the 1st of run 12). The span of the `[patch]` entry is the same, so Talon applies its edit to the new text (R-CC7): `talon.toml` has the comment of the editor and the new `base`, the header is written, and the copy is replaced.
Run 13 (p10, `add acme/util --no-check`): the patch is stale (the header has json 2.2.0, the base is json 2.1.0), and `tln add` has a name, so it does not rebase the patch (only `tln install` with no names does, R-PA6; question Q6). The output has the new entry only. The calls have no read of `packages/<base>` and no temp root of a patch, and `tree.diff` has the new header line and the `projects.json` line only: the copy and `base` stay.
